    {
      "id": "corroboration-cannot-outrank-the-stronger-kind",
      "title": "Five tools naming one account do not make it a stronger bridge than one shared artifact",
      "rationale": "Confidence answers two different questions at once — how strong is this KIND of link, and how many independent tools corroborate it — and the second must never overturn the first. The old scale summed a kind weight of up to 1.0 with a corroboration bonus of up to 0.3 and then clamped to 1.0, so both of the bridges below came out at exactly 1.00 and the tie fell through to the family count: the account, seen by five tools, was ranked ABOVE the hash, seen by two. That is the reverse of the hierarchy the module documents, and it is what an analyst reads first. The hash here is deliberately the weakest it can be (exactly two families) and the account the strongest (five, all spelling it identically, so no normalization penalty applies): if corroboration can cross a band at all, it crosses it here.",
